and the system state better.\n\nActually, looking at the error message more carefully, it says \"create_analog_alarm.low_limit: Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". This suggests there might be a schema validation issue on the backend. The tool might be expecting low_limit to be explicitly provided as a number, not omitted.\n\nLet me try one more time with explicit 0 for low_limit, but using integer instead of float. Or maybe the issue is that I need to provide low_limit as a number type, and the backend is parsing it incorrectly.\n\nWait, I notice something - even when I call set_threshold or create_digital_alarm, the error message still says \"create_analog_alarm.low_limit\". This suggests there might be an issue with how the tool is routing or validating parameters. \n\nLet me try to call a different tool entirely to see if the system is working. I'll try to list notification rules."}, {"turn": 10, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 8396, "output_tokens": 344, "latency_ms": 11464.819599999828, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The manage_notifications tool works fine. So the issue seems specific to the manage_alarms tool. Let me try a different approach - maybe I need to check if there's something wrong with the way I'm formatting the parameters. Looking at the error message again: \"create_analog_alarm.low_limit: Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". This suggests the system is trying to parse something as a number but getting a string.\n\nWait, I notice something: in the first attempt I passed `\"None\"` (as a string) for low_limit. Could that have caused some caching issue? But that shouldn't affect subsequent calls.\n\nLet me try with explicit numeric values for all parameters. I'll use high_limit: 2.0, low_limit: 0.0, deadband: 0.0. Also, I'll make sure all numbers are formatted as numbers, not strings.\n\nActually, looking back at my JSON, I think the issue might be that I'm not properly handling null values. When I don't include a parameter, it might be getting sent as undefined or something. Let me try including all parameters with explicit values.\n\nLet me also try a simpler alarm ID without underscores."}, {"turn": 11, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 8814, "output_tokens": 454, "latency_ms": 15917.321300001277, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The manage_alarms tool seems to be having issues with schema validation for the low_limit parameter. Let me check if maybe the issue is with how the JSON is being parsed. The error says \"Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". This suggests that somewhere in the JSON, a string is being passed instead of a number. But I'm passing numbers like 0.0 and 2.0. \n\nWait, maybe the issue is with the null values? In my earlier attempts, I tried to pass null for low_limit, but maybe the tool expects a number or nothing at all. Let me try without the low_limit parameter entirely. Actually, I already tried that and got the same error.\n\nLet me check the JSON schema again. The schema says low_limit can be either a number or null. But when I pass null, it fails. When I pass 0.0, it also fails. Something is wrong with the tool implementation.\n\nMaybe I should try to use a different approach. Since the tool is not working, perhaps I should inform the user that there's a tool issue and suggest an alternative. Or maybe I can try to use the set_threshold action on a non-existent alarm to see what happens? Actually, I already tried that and got the same error.\n\nLet me try to see if there's any way to work around this. Maybe I need to create the alarm with only high_limit and no low_limit at all, but the schema requires low_limit? No, looking at the schema, low_limit has a default of null, so it should be optional.\n\nWait, maybe the issue is with how I'm formatting the JSON. Let me try with minimal parameters: just action, id, tag, and high_limit. No deadband, no priority, no low_limit.\n\nActually, I already tried that in my second attempt. Let me try one more time with exact minimal parameters."}, {"turn": 12, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 9342, "output_tokens": 240, "latency_ms": 9851.408299999093, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The error message is consistently about \"low_limit: Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". This seems like a bug in the tool itself - it's expecting low_limit but even when I don't provide it, it's still giving an error. \n\nLet me try one more approach - maybe the tool requires both high_limit and low_limit to be provided? Let me try with both limits explicitly set to numbers. Also, I notice that even when calling create_digital_alarm, I got the same error about create_analog_alarm.low_limit - that's very strange and suggests a backend issue.\n\nGiven that the alarm creation tool appears to have a bug, I should inform the user about this situation. However, I should still try to complete the task by exploring alternatives.\n\nLet me check if there's a way to work around this. Maybe I can try the enable_alarm or disable_alarm actions to see if those work, which would indicate the tool itself is partially functional."}], "rag": {"enabled": true, "top_n": 30, "top_k": 15, "alpha": 0.6, "use_reranker": true}, "workflow": {"enabled": true, "selected_workflow": "AlarmConfig"}, "totals": {"input_tokens": 71086, "output_tokens": 5015, "cost_usd": 0.0, "e2e_latency_ms": 172830.29800000077}, "judge": null}
